{"from": "site:kursi", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-05-01-gadarenes-region", "grade": "D", "note": "Kursi's Byzantine monastery is traditionally identified as the Gergesa site of the miracle, but the identification is disputed: Gergasa is not known before late antiquity and does not appear in early manuscripts, and pre-Origen texts call the area the Land of the Gadarenes."}
{"from": "site:gadara-ancient-tombs", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-05-02-03-one-demoniac", "grade": "C", "note": "Early visitors to Umm Qais (Gadara) recorded 'the tombs and other monuments still visible on the surface' - rock-cut tombs in the country Mark names, from which the man could come out, though not a record of this man."}
{"from": "site:gadara-ancient-tombs", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-05-03-05-unbindable-cutting", "grade": "C", "note": "The same tombs around Gadara show the kind of abandoned sepulchres in which a homeless man could dwell; the chains, the crying out and the self-cutting have no fetched evidence."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-3-fifth-tenth-legions", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-05-09-legion-named", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus: Titus fetched 'the fifth and the tenth legions' into Syria (66 CE) - legio was the standing term for a Roman unit of thousands, and the tenth (X Fretensis) served in Syria from c. 6 CE (Wikipedia garrison list 'Syria (c. 6 – 66)', mascots including the boar). This attests the word's military reality, not the exorcism."}
{"from": "site:kursi", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-05-11-13-two-thousand-swine", "grade": "D", "note": "The same traditional identification marks the steep slope to the lake at Kursi as the swine-miracle site; the monastery is Byzantine and attests the commemorative tradition, not the herd or its number."}
{"from": "artifact:theodotos-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-05-22-23-jairus-office", "grade": "C", "note": "The Theodotos inscription (1st-century Jerusalem) names 'a synagogue' with its archisynagogos family: the office Mark gives Jairus existed in the same period, though the inscription is from Jerusalem, not Galilee."}
